.\"
.\" SEM_INIT(3) - Substrate Pthreads Library
.\"
.TH SEM_INIT 3 "2026-10-19" "Substrate OS" "Library Functions"
.SH NAME
sem_init, sem_destroy \- initialise and destroy an unnamed semaphore
.SH LIBRARY
POSIX threads library
.RI ( libpthread ", " \-lpthread )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <semaphore.h>
.PP
.BI "int sem_init(sem_t *" sem ", int " pshared ", unsigned int " value ");"
.BI "int sem_destroy(sem_t *" sem ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR sem_init ()
initialises the unnamed semaphore at
.I sem
with the count
.IR value ,
for use with
.BR sem_wait (3),
.BR sem_post (3)
and
.BR sem_getvalue (3).
.PP
With
.I pshared
equal to 0 the semaphore is shared only by the threads of the calling
process.  It is a futex word inside
.I sem
and stays in user space until a thread has to block.
.PP
With
.I pshared
nonzero the semaphore may be shared between processes.  It is backed by a
kernel semaphore
.RB ( ksem_init (2))
bound to the memory
.I sem
lives in, so
.I sem
should be in memory the processes share: a
.B MAP_SHARED
mapping of the same file or shared-memory object, a shared anonymous
mapping inherited across
.BR fork (2),
or an attached System V shared-memory segment.  A process may use the
semaphore when it maps that memory, whatever address it maps it at, and a
child forked after
.BR sem_init ()
may use it through the reference it inherits.  No other process can,
even if it learns the kernel's identifier for it.
.PP
.BR sem_destroy ()
destroys an unnamed semaphore.  For a process-shared one it drops the
calling process's reference; the kernel object goes away once no process
holds one.  Destroying a semaphore other threads are blocked on is
undefined.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Both return 0 on success.  On failure they return \-1 and set
.I errno
to indicate the error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EFAULT
.RB ( sem_init ()
with
.I pshared
nonzero)
.I sem
is not a mapped address.
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I sem
is
.BR NULL ,
.I value
exceeds
.BR SEM_VALUE_MAX ,
or
.RB ( sem_destroy ())
.I sem
is a named semaphore, which is closed with
.BR sem_close (3)
instead.
.TP
.B ENOSPC
.I pshared
is nonzero and the system-wide limit on kernel semaphores would be
exceeded, or
.I pshared
is 0 and the process already has
.B SEM_NSEMS_MAX
unnamed semaphores.
.SH EXAMPLE
A parent and its child share a semaphore in a shared anonymous mapping:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <sys/mman.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main(void)
{
    sem_t *s = mmap(NULL, sizeof(*s), PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE,
                    MAP_SHARED | MAP_ANONYMOUS, -1, 0);
    sem_init(s, 1, 0);
    if (fork() == 0) {
        sem_post(s);        /* wake the parent */
        _exit(0);
    }
    sem_wait(s);            /* blocks until the child posts */
    wait(NULL);
    sem_destroy(s);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sem_open (3),
.BR sem_wait (3),
.BR sem_post (3),
.BR sem_getvalue (3),
.BR ksem_open (2),
.BR mmap (2),
.BR shmat (2)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2017.
